    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d874">

                <pb facs="1005" n="811" xml:id="pg811"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 EW/7–2345</note>
                <head>No. 874<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Chief of the Division of Central
                        European Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Riddleberger</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Assistant Secretary of State</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Dunn</hi>)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d874-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Babelsberg</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1945-07-23">July 23, 1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Memorandum for Mr. Dunn</hi></p>
                <p>In connection with Molotov’s proposal<anchor n="2" xml:id="d874-f2"/> that
                    payment for a minimum import program approved by the Control Council would be
                    the first charge on German exports, the following should be borne in mind with
                    reference to the powers of the Control Council:</p>
                <p>Article 1 of the Agreement on Control Machinery in Germany<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d874-f3"/> stipulates that supreme authority will be exercised by
                    the four Commanders in Chief, <hi rendition="#italic">each in his own zone of
                        occupation</hi>, and also jointly in matters affecting Germany as a whole in
                    their capacity as members of the Control Council. Article 3 (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">c</hi>), however, stipulates that decisions of the
                    Control Council must be unanimous. This means, of course, that any minimum
                    import program would have to be approved by the unanimous decision of the
                    Control Council. In the absence of unanimity in the Council, the authority of
                    the zone commanders would obviously remain supreme.</p>
                <p>It can therefore be assumed that if there be no agreement in the Control Council
                    on a minimum import program, the American zone commander would not have to make
                    reparation deliveries from the U. S. zone. However, this point should no doubt
                    be made very clear to Molotov if we contemplate accepting his proposal.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
